  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Magnitude Spectrum Under Time Shifting",
    "lesson_core_move": "Recognize that time shifting a periodic signal leaves each Fourier series magnitude |$c_k$| unchanged because the time-shift multiplier only rotates coefficient phase.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude Spectrum Under Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Magnitude Spectrum Under Time Shifting",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For one harmonic coefficient $c_k$ of a periodic signal, a time shift keeps the harmonic index fixed and changes only the coefficient phase. For $x(t-t_0)$, the shifted coefficient is $c_k' = c_k e^{-jk\\omega_0 t_0}$, so the first thing to recognize is the phase-rotation factor attached to the original coefficient.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_k' = c_k e^{-jk\\omega_0 t_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The multiplier $e^{-jk\\omega_0 t_0}$ has unit magnitude, so it rotates $c_k$ in the complex plane without changing its length. That is the key cue: a time shift can move phase, but it cannot change the size of the coefficient.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|c_k'| = |c_k|\\,|e^{-jk\\omega_0 t_0}| = |c_k|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the magnitude spectrum stays the same for every harmonic index $k$, even though the phase spectrum changes. The shift changes where each coefficient points, not how tall each magnitude bar is.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this rule whenever a periodic signal is time shifted: multiply each coefficient by the phase factor, then read the magnitudes as unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude Spectrum Under Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Unit-Magnitude Time-Shift Multiplier",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal has Fourier coefficient $c_3 = 2 - 2j$. After the shift $x(t-t_0)$, the coefficient becomes $c_3' = c_3 e^{-j3\\omega_0 t_0}$. Identify the multiplier's magnitude and the magnitude of $c_3'$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the factor multiplying $c_3$ in the shifted coefficient. For $x(t-t_0)$, the shift does not change the harmonic index; it appends the multiplier $e^{-j3\\omega_0 t_0}$ to the original coefficient.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_3' = c_3 e^{-j3\\omega_0 t_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $e^{-j3\\omega_0 t_0}$ is a complex exponential, its magnitude is $1$. So the shift changes phase only, not coefficient size.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|e^{-j3\\omega_0 t_0}| = 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compute the original magnitude: $|c_3| = |2-2j| = \\sqrt{2^2+(-2)^2} = 2\\sqrt{2}$. Since the multiplier has unit magnitude, the shifted coefficient keeps that same magnitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|c_3'| = |c_3| = 2\\sqrt{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the multiplier is unit-magnitude, and the shifted coefficient has magnitude $2\\sqrt{2}$. The phase changes, but the magnitude spectrum height does not.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude Spectrum Under Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding Magnitudes After a Time Shift",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A signal has coefficients $c_0 = 2$, $c_1 = 1 - j$, and $c_2 = -3j$. After the shift $x(t-t_0)$, determine the shifted magnitudes $|c_0'|$, $|c_1'|$, and $|c_2'|$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the time-shift factor on each coefficient. A shift leaves the coefficient list in place and multiplies each harmonic by a complex exponential with magnitude $1$, so the magnitudes should match the original list.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_0' = c_0,\\qquad c_1' = c_1 e^{-j\\omega_0 t_0},\\qquad c_2' = c_2 e^{-j2\\omega_0 t_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the original magnitudes: $|c_0| = |2| = 2$, $|c_1| = |1-j| = \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2} = \\sqrt{2}$, and $|c_2| = |-3j| = 3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|c_0'| = |c_0| = 2,\\qquad |c_1'| = |c_1| = \\sqrt{2},\\qquad |c_2'| = |c_2| = 3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the shifted magnitude list is identical to the original one: the Fourier-series spectrum heights do not change under time shifting. Only the phases rotate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude Spectrum Under Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Comparing Original and Shifted Magnitude Spectra",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "The original magnitude spectrum is $\\{|c_0|, |c_1|, |c_2|\\} = \\{4, 1, 2\\}$. After shifting the signal to $x(t-t_0)$, which magnitude spectrum is correct?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the full magnitude list, not the complex coefficients. A time shift changes each coefficient by a unit-magnitude factor, so each entry in the spectrum keeps its height.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\{|c_0|, |c_1|, |c_2|\\} = \\{4, 1, 2\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the shift rule term by term: $|c_0'| = |c_0| = 4$, $|c_1'| = |c_1| = 1$, and $|c_2'| = |c_2| = 2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\{|c_0'|, |c_1'|, |c_2'|\\} = \\{4, 1, 2\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the correct shifted magnitude spectrum is the same ordered list as the original one. The coefficients rotate in phase, but the spectrum heights do not move or scale.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Magnitude Spectrum Under Time Shifting",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Distinguishing Magnitude Invariance from Phase Rotation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A student says the magnitude spectrum must change because $c_k' = c_k e^{-jk\\omega_0 t_0}$. Decide what changes and what stays the same for $x(t-t_0)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the update rule itself. A time shift does not rewrite the coefficient list; it multiplies each $c_k$ by a complex exponential that depends on $k$, so the only question is whether that multiplier changes magnitude or only phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_k' = c_k e^{-jk\\omega_0 t_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The exponential factor has magnitude $1$, so it cannot change $|c_k|$. It only rotates the phase by an amount that depends on $k$ and the shift $t_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|c_k'| = |c_k|,\\qquad \\angle c_k' = \\angle c_k - k\\omega_0 t_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the student's claim is false. A time shift changes phase, not magnitude: the magnitude spectrum stays fixed while the phase spectrum rotates.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
